Sure, here's an example of how to generate a table of contents (TOC) automatically in a LaTeX document:

1. First, add the following lines to your preamble (before `\begin{document}`):

   ```
   \documentclass{article} % or book, report, etc.

   \usepackage{tocloft} % optional, for customizing TOC appearance

   \begin{document}
   ```

   Note that the `tocloft` package is optional and can be used to customize the appearance of the TOC. If you don't need customization, you can omit this line.

2. Add the `\tableofcontents` command after the `\begin{document}` line:

   ```
   \documentclass{article}

   \usepackage{tocloft}

   \begin{document}
     \tableofcontents
   ```

   This command generates the TOC based on the sections, subsections, and other headings defined in your document.

3. To include the TOC in your document, add the following line to your preamble:

   ```
   \documentclass{article}

   \usepackage{tocloft}

   \begin{document}
     \tableofcontents
   ```

   ```
   \end{document}
   ```

   This line tells LaTeX to include the TOC at the beginning of your document.

4. Add section headings to your document using standard LaTeX commands, such as `\section`, `\subsection`, etc.

   ```
   \documentclass{article}

   \usepackage{tocloft}

   \begin{document}
     \tableofcontents

     \section{First Section}
     \subsection{First Subsection}
     \subsubsection{First Subsubsection}

     \section{Second Section}
     \subsection{Second Subsection}
     \subsubsection{Second Subsubsection}

     \section{Third Section}
     \subsection{Third Subsection}
     \subsubsection{Third Subsubsection}

   \end{document}
   ```

   The TOC will automatically include all the section headings you define in your document.

5. Compile your LaTeX document using your preferred toolchain (e.g., LaTeX, pdfLaTeX, XeLaTeX, etc.). The resulting PDF will include an automatically generated TOC at the beginning of the document.

That's it! With these steps, you should have a working table of contents in your LaTeX document. Note that you can further customize the appearance and behavior of the TOC using additional packages and commands, but this example covers the basics.

