In the case of comparing strings, `==` operator checks whether the two string objects being compared refer to the same instance in memory. This means that `s1 == s2` will return `true` only if `s1` and `s2` are the same object, i.e., they have the same memory address. This is not usually what you want when comparing strings for equality.

On the other hand, the `.equals()` method is the preferred way to compare strings for value equality in Java. This method is defined in the `java.lang.String` class and compares the characters of the two strings character by character to determine if they have the same sequence of characters.

In general, you should use the `.equals()` method to compare strings in Java, unless you have a specific reason to check for reference equality. Here are some guidelines to follow:

- Use `==` to compare string literals, as they are guaranteed to refer to the same object in memory. For example, `"hello".equals("hello")` will always return `true`.
- Use `.equals()` to compare non-literal strings, as they may refer to different objects in memory, even if they contain the same characters.

Here's an example to illustrate the difference:

```java
String s1 = "hello";
String s2 = "hello";

// Using == operator to compare strings
if (s1 == s2) {
    System.out.println("s1 and s2 refer to the same object");
} else {
    System.out.println("s1 and s2 refer to different objects");
}

// Using .equals() method to compare strings
if (s1.equals(s2)) {
    System.out.println("s1 and s2 have the same value");
} else {
    System.out.println("s1 and s2 have different values");
}
```

